From: SamuriHL <samurihl@msn.com>
Subject: bdj: deliver the reader's timing notifications at presentation time

A player buffers ahead of the picture - Kodi reads up to 16 s ahead - and
libbluray hands the BD-J application its timing notifications as the
reader produces them: play marks, playitem and chapter changes, end of
playlist, and the PSR 8 presentation time. The application then lives up
to the buffer depth in the future. Measured on M3GAN 2.0: its menu
background (a 4 h loop, 243 repeats of one clip) was read 14 s ahead of
the screen, and the application's notion of where playback was ran that
far ahead of what the viewer saw. A licensed player keeps the application
on the presentation clock.

Add opt-in presentation timing for BD-J titles. When enabled with
bd_bdj_set_presentation_timing(), the reader's timing notifications and
PSR 8 writes are held in a numbered queue (consecutive time updates
collapse). The player stamps bd_bdj_pending_seq() with its read position
and calls bd_bdj_release() when the picture reaches it. The application's
media clock (Libbluray.tellTime) reports the presented position.

PSR 5 (chapter) and 7 (playitem) are still WRITTEN at read time - the
reader, the graphics controller and the player's own BD_EVENT_PLAYITEM and
BD_EVENT_CHAPTER depend on that - but while notifications are held the
application reads their presented values (the last PLAYITEM/CHAPTER event
released to it), so PSR 5/7 stay consistent with the held PSR 8 instead of a
whole playitem ahead of it.

Unchanged: HDMV titles, user input, register writes by the application,
rate changes, and the stream-number PSRs the reader validates when a clip
opens (audio, PG, IG): those drive the reader's own stream handling and only
change where a clip's STN table differs, so the application sees such a change
up to the read-ahead early. Everything held is released first on a seek, a playlist
close and a BD-J start/stop/close, so a mixed disc's HDMV title reads the
registers the application actually saw. Disabled by default; a player
that never enables it gets libbluray's existing behaviour.

